  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1246 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nMid-range ensembles are in good agreement at status quo regarding\npattern evolution through the remainder of the week. Deep longwave\ntrough will sit between Hudson Bay and New England with amplified\nflow persisting across the Central Plains in much of the Midwest.\nEventual ridge breakdown should occur over the weekend as a couple\nmore pronounced systems approach the western CONUS. These features\nshould phase together as they progress east across the Great Basin\nand towards the High Plains. Southerly flow including a greater fetch\nof moisture from the Gulf should be established by Saturday and\nwill promote moderating temperatures towards the end of the forecast\nperiod. Both EPS and GEFS solutions depict modest lee cyclogenesis\ndeveloping by early Sunday to our southwest and subtle lift may be\nenough to produce at least some light precipitation for mainly\ncentral and eastern Nebraska. Temperatures appear quite mild and\nshould support all rain with any precipitation that does occur though\ndaytime temperatures may falter some with the increased cloudiness\nand rain. Confidence in precise details, particularly late weekend\ninto early next week, varies considerably between ensemble solutions.\nNBM inner-quartile spread in daytime maximum temperatures doubles\nfrom Friday to Sunday (9F to 19F) at North Platte. Though ensembles\ngenerally agree on timing and progression of upper-level wave pattern,\nsome subtle timing and placement discrepancies remain. Overall\nthough, following a fairly light QPF event late weekend into early\nthe following week, a brief lull aligning with another shortwave\nridging setup briefly return before even greater precipitation\npotential returns. Ensembles show greater certainty in occurrence as\nwell as greater potential magnitude of moisture: 30-40%+ of\nindividual members painting 0.1\" or greater total liquid equivalent\nfrom both the EPS and GEFS solutions. Similarly, temperatures appear\nlikely to remain mild enough to preclude much if any wintry\nprecipitation types but this will need monitored. Recurring troughing\nover the western CONUS should support an active pattern through mid\nand late month, an idea highlighted by the Climate Prediction\nCenter's latest outlooks. Given the time of year, should any influx\nof more Arctic air be realized, precipitation may support greater\npotential for wintry weather. This will be especially true as\nguidance hints at a notable system crossing the region in the days\nleading up to the Thanksgiving holiday so stay tuned.",
